WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.514
Hello, everyone! I'm Mr. Daniel. Welcome to Online Upper-Intermediate.

2
00:00:04.814 --> 00:00:07.736
Hi! I'm Nita. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.036 --> 00:00:14.450
Unit one: Making Connections. In this unit, you will talk about meeting people and building relationships.

4
00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:16.381
rapport.

5
00:00:17.081 --> 00:00:20.834
rapport means a friendly relationship in which people understand each other.

6
00:00:21.134 --> 00:00:23.819
She quickly built a good rapport with her new clients.

7
00:00:24.519 --> 00:00:26.695
Your turn. Say: rapport.

8
00:00:29.395 --> 00:00:30.222
rapport.

9
00:00:31.217 --> 00:00:32.311
common ground.

10
00:00:33.011 --> 00:00:36.508
common ground means interests or opinions that two people share.

11
00:00:36.808 --> 00:00:39.063
We found common ground in our love of football.

12
00:00:39.763 --> 00:00:42.508
Your turn. Say: common ground.

13
00:00:45.208 --> 00:00:46.314
common ground.

14
00:00:47.317 --> 00:00:48.445
break the ice.

15
00:00:49.145 --> 00:00:53.095
break the ice means to say or do something to make people feel relaxed.

16
00:00:53.395 --> 00:00:55.697
He told a funny story to break the ice.

17
00:00:56.397 --> 00:00:58.852
Your turn. Say: break the ice.

18
00:01:01.552 --> 00:01:02.669
break the ice.

19
00:01:03.683 --> 00:01:04.557
follow up.

20
00:01:05.257 --> 00:01:08.580
follow up means to contact someone again after a first meeting.

21
00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.355
I'll follow up with an email after the conference.

22
00:01:12.055 --> 00:01:14.081
Your turn. Say: follow up.

23
00:01:16.781 --> 00:01:17.677
follow up.

24
00:01:18.683 --> 00:01:19.766
mutual friend.

25
00:01:20.466 --> 00:01:23.696
mutual friend means a person who is a friend of both of you.

26
00:01:23.996 --> 00:01:26.053
We met through a mutual friend at a wedding.

27
00:01:26.753 --> 00:01:29.069
Your turn. Say: mutual friend.

28
00:01:31.769 --> 00:01:33.037
mutual friend.

29
00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:35.016
icebreaker.

30
00:01:35.716 --> 00:01:39.457
icebreaker means a game or activity that helps people get to know each other.

31
00:01:39.757 --> 00:01:41.815
The trainer started with a fun icebreaker.

32
00:01:42.515 --> 00:01:45.028
Your turn. Say: icebreaker.

33
00:01:47.728 --> 00:01:48.833
icebreaker.

34
00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:50.572
introvert.

35
00:01:51.272 --> 00:01:54.863
introvert means a quiet person who prefers small groups and time alone.

36
00:01:55.163 --> 00:01:58.242
As an introvert, networking events tire me out.

37
00:01:58.942 --> 00:02:01.211
Your turn. Say: introvert.

38
00:02:03.911 --> 00:02:04.901
introvert.

39
00:02:05.917 --> 00:02:06.906
extrovert.

40
00:02:07.606 --> 00:02:11.661
extrovert means an outgoing person who enjoys being with lots of people.

41
00:02:11.961 --> 00:02:15.365
Dara is a real extrovert: he talks to everyone.

42
00:02:16.065 --> 00:02:18.230
Your turn. Say: extrovert.

43
00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:22.082
extrovert.

44
00:02:23.083 --> 00:02:25.164
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:25.464 --> 00:02:28.871
How's the new job going, Nita? Have you been making connections?

46
00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:34.763
Yes! I've been meeting so many people, although I'm more of an introvert.

47
00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:38.830
That's natural. How did you break the ice with your team?

48
00:02:40.117 --> 00:02:44.009
We found some common ground: half the office loves badminton!

49
00:02:45.317 --> 00:02:49.118
Excellent. And have you followed up with the manager you met last week?

50
00:02:50.417 --> 00:02:55.925
Not yet, but I'm writing to her tomorrow. We have a mutual friend, so it's easier.

51
00:02:57.217 --> 00:02:59.797
Grammar focus: Present tenses review.

52
00:03:00.097 --> 00:03:01.945
Let's review the present tenses.

53
00:03:02.245 --> 00:03:10.805
Present simple for facts and routines, present continuous for now and temporary situations, and present perfect for actions connecting the past to now.

54
00:03:11.105 --> 00:03:15.449
For example: I've been working here since March, and I've made three friends.

55
00:03:15.749 --> 00:03:20.651
At B2, choose the tense to show your meaning: permanent, temporary, or connected to now.

56
00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:24.488
Now read and listen. The Science of Small Talk.

57
00:03:24.788 --> 00:03:29.841
Many people dislike small talk, but research suggests it is more valuable than we think.

58
00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:34.868
Brief conversations with strangers have been shown to improve our mood.

59
00:03:36.183 --> 00:03:40.958
Introverts often find these moments tiring, while extroverts tend to enjoy them.

60
00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:46.363
The key is finding common ground quickly, whether it's the weather, sport or food.

61
00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:51.461
A simple question can break the ice and build rapport surprisingly fast.

62
00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:55.205
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

63
00:03:55.505 --> 00:03:59.095
A friendly relationship where people understand each other is blank.

64
00:03:59.395 --> 00:04:03.466
A, rapport. B, an icebreaker. C, a mutual friend.

65
00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:09.090
The answer is A: rapport.

66
00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:13.233
I blank here for three years, and I still love it.

67
00:04:13.533 --> 00:04:16.861
A, work. B, have been working. C, am working.

68
00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:23.082
The answer is B: have been working.

69
00:04:24.583 --> 00:04:26.745
These days, more people blank from home.

70
00:04:27.045 --> 00:04:30.084
A, work. B, are working. C, have worked.

71
00:04:34.083 --> 00:04:36.141
The answer is B: are working.

72
00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:40.056
To make people feel relaxed is to blank.

73
00:04:40.356 --> 00:04:44.450
A, follow up. B, break the ice. C, find common ground.

74
00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:50.577
The answer is B: break the ice.

75
00:04:52.083 --> 00:04:55.546
According to the text, brief conversations with strangers can blank.

76
00:04:55.846 --> 00:04:59.778
A, improve our mood. B, waste time. C, make us tired.

77
00:05:03.783 --> 00:05:05.574
The answer is A: improve our mood.

78
00:05:07.083 --> 00:05:11.071
Now it's your turn to speak. How do you usually break the ice when you meet someone new?

79
00:05:16.371 --> 00:05:21.714
I usually ask about something we have in common, like the event we're at, and I try to listen more than I talk.

80
00:05:22.014 --> 00:05:23.387
Excellent answer, Nita!

81
00:05:24.383 --> 00:05:28.336
Great work today! You learned eight new words and present tenses review.

82
00:05:28.636 --> 00:05:31.393
Today's value is kindness. Be kind to everyone.

83
00:05:31.693 --> 00:05:33.811
See you in Unit two. Goodbye!

84
00:05:34.111 --> 00:05:36.810
Thank you, Mr. Daniel! Bye, everyone!

